    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm studying for my circuits final and im kinda stuck. can u explain the main difference in how a capacitor behaves when it's in a DC circuit vs an AC circuit? also what's the formula for capacitive reactance? thanks",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "In a DC circuit, a capacitor acts like an open switch once it is fully charged, blocking the steady flow of direct current. Conversely, in an AC circuit, the capacitor allows the current to fluctuate, effectively passing the alternating signal. The formula used to calculate the opposition to AC current in a capacitor, known as capacitive reactance ($\\text{X}_\\text{C}$), is $\\text{X}_\\text{C} = 1 / (2\\pi f\\text{C})$.",
