    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "that makes sense. so with the AC part, why does the frequency matter for the reactance? like, if the frequency gets really high, what happens to the capacitor's resistance to the current?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "As frequency increases in an AC circuit, the capacitor's reactance ($\\text{X}_\\text{C}$) decreases because frequency is in the denominator of the reactance formula. This means that at very high frequencies, a capacitor offers very little opposition to the current flow. Essentially, at extremely high frequencies, a capacitor begins to behave more like a short circuit, letting most of the AC signal pass through.",
      "source": "target",
