    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "got it. so it's basically like the capacitor becomes less of an obstacle as the signal cycles faster. can you explain what's actually happening at the molecular or charge level during those cycles that causes this change in reactance?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "At the charge level, the capacitance is the ability to store charge, and the faster the signal cycles, the less time the capacitor has to fully accumulate or discharge the charge on its plates. This rapid charging and discharging allows the changing voltage to \"push\" charges across the dielectric more quickly. Therefore, at high frequencies, the current leads the voltage because the capacitor has less time to effectively oppose the rapid changes.",
